@node Configuration
@chapter Configuration
@cindex configuration

Codebud's settings come from environment variables and an optional
configuration file, @file{config.toml}; the safety policy comes from three
JSON policy files.  This chapter documents all of them, and where Codebud
keeps its files.

@menu
* Environment Variables Overview::  Variables that control Codebud.
* Configuration File::              The same settings in config.toml.
* LLM Backend Variables::           Choosing Ollama or OpenAI.
* Privacy Levels::                  locked, private, trusted.
* Profiles::                        Mode, privacy, policy and prompt in one name.
* Isolated Steps::                  No network, project-only writes, secrets hidden.
* Feature Flags::                   Enabling optional features.
* Policy JSON Files::               The three safety configuration files.
* Harmless Commands::               Commands always allowed.
* Powerful Commands::               Commands allowed with care.
* Trusted Sequences::               Named multi-step sequences.
* Checking Your Configuration::     Using codebud config.
* Files and Directories::           Where data and policy files live.
@end menu


@node Environment Variables Overview
@section Environment Variables Overview
@cindex environment variables

The table below lists every variable Codebud reads, its default, and what
it does.

@cindex yes/no values
Variables that switch something on or off accept @code{1}, @code{true},
@code{yes} or @code{on}, and @code{0}, @code{false}, @code{no} or
@code{off}, in any case; unset or empty means the default.  Any other
value is warned about and taken the safe way: protective switches
(@env{CODEBUD_ENCRYPT}, @env{CODEBUD_MASK_SECRETS}) stay on, switches
that let data leave the machine (@env{WEB_SEARCH_ENABLED},
@env{CODEBUD_ALLOW_REMOTE_HTTP}) stay off.

@multitable @columnfractions .25 .25 .50
@headitem Variable @tab Default @tab Purpose
@item @env{OLLAMA_MODEL}
@tab @emph{(none)}
@tab Model name exactly as it appears in @command{ollama list}.
Required for the Ollama backend.  Can be overridden per invocation
without touching your shell profile --- see @option{--model}/@option{-m}
and @code{/model} in the Usage chapter (@pxref{The run Subcommand}).
@item @env{OLLAMA_BASE_URL}
@tab @code{http://localhost:11434}
@tab Base URL of the Ollama REST API.
@item @env{CODEBUD_NUM_CTX}
@tab @code{8192}
@tab Context window, in tokens, that Codebud requests from Ollama on
every call.  Ollama's own default (4096) is small enough that long
prompts lose their beginning, which holds the planner's instructions.
The same value is sent on every request, because a change makes Ollama
reload the model and discard its cached prompt prefix.  Larger values use
more memory (roughly 36 KB per token for a 3B Qwen2.5 model).  Invalid
values are ignored with a warning.
@item @env{EMBED_MODEL}
@tab @code{all-minilm}
@tab Dedicated embedding model used by @command{codebud index} and the
@code{search_embeddings} tool.  It is independent of @env{OLLAMA_MODEL};
a text-generation model makes poor embeddings.  Pull it with
@command{ollama pull all-minilm}.  @code{nomic-embed-text} reads longer
chunks and other languages better but indexes about eight times slower on
a CPU (@pxref{Project Search Evaluation}).  Models that expect task prefixes
get them automatically: @code{nomic-embed-text} (@samp{search_document:} /
@samp{search_query:}) and @code{qwen3-embedding} (an instruction on
queries).
@item @env{CODEBUD_ALLOW_REMOTE_HTTP}
@tab @code{0}
@tab Set to @code{1} to allow a remote @env{OLLAMA_BASE_URL} over plain
http (prompts unencrypted on the network).  Remote https and local servers
need nothing (@pxref{LLM Backend Variables}).
@item @env{CODEBUD_MASK_SECRETS}
@tab @code{1}
@tab Mask secrets (API keys, tokens, private keys, passwords) as
@samp{[redacted]} in saved sessions and step history.  @code{0} turns it
off (@pxref{Files and Directories}).
@item @env{CODEBUD_ENCRYPT}
@tab @code{0}
@tab Set to @code{1} to encrypt chat sessions, the step history and new
project indexes with
your age key (@pxref{Encryption at Rest}); create the key first with
@command{codebud key init}.
@item @env{CODEBUD_STEP_ENV}
@tab (empty)
@tab More environment variables for command steps, which otherwise get
only an allow-list (@pxref{Isolated Steps}): names or @code{PREFIX_*}
patterns, space or comma separated.
@item @env{CODEBUD_PASSPHRASE}
@tab (not set)
@tab The passphrase of the encryption key, for unattended runs; otherwise
it is asked on the terminal.  Environment only, never @file{config.toml};
@command{codebud doctor} warns while it is set (@pxref{Encryption at
Rest}).
@item @env{CODEBUD_PRIVACY}
@tab @code{private}
@tab What Codebud may reach: @code{locked}, @code{private} or
@code{trusted} (@pxref{Privacy Levels}).
@item @env{CODEBUD_PROFILE}
@tab @code{default}
@tab The profile to start with (@pxref{Profiles}).
@item @env{CODEBUD_SESSION_RETENTION_DAYS}
@tab @code{0}
@tab Delete chat sessions not used for this many days when a chat starts.
@code{0} keeps them forever (@pxref{The sessions Subcommand}).
@item @env{CODEBUD_BACKEND}
@tab @code{ollama}
@tab Which LLM backend gets the prompts: @code{ollama} or @code{openai}.
There is no fallback between them (@pxref{LLM Backend Variables}).
@item @env{OPENAI_API_KEY}
@tab @emph{(none)}
@tab API key for the @code{openai} backend; used only when
@env{CODEBUD_BACKEND} is @code{openai} (requires @code{codebud[openai]}).
@item @env{OPENAI_MODEL}
@tab @code{gpt-3.5-turbo}
@tab OpenAI model to use when the OpenAI backend is active.
@item @env{CODEBUD_RAG}
@tab @code{1}
@tab Set to @code{0} to leave project context (from @command{codebud
index}) out of the planning prompt.
@item @env{CODEBUD_RAG_HINTS}
@tab @code{1}
@tab Set to @code{0} to stop @command{chat} from suggesting @code{/index}
when the project index is missing or out of date.
@item @env{WEB_SEARCH_ENABLED}
@tab @code{0}
@tab Set to @code{1} to enable the @code{web_search} tool.  When
@code{0}, the tool reports itself as unavailable to the planner.
@item @env{XDG_DATA_HOME}
@tab @file{~/.local/share}
@tab Codebud keeps its runtime data in @file{$XDG_DATA_HOME/codebud}
(@pxref{Files and Directories}).
@item @env{XDG_CONFIG_HOME}
@tab @file{~/.config}
@tab Your own policy files go in @file{$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/codebud}
(@pxref{Policy JSON Files}).
@item @env{CODEBUD_LEGACY_DATA_DIR}
@tab @emph{(the checkout's @file{data/})}
@tab Where versions before October 2026 kept runtime data; copied once into
the data directory.  Rarely needed (the test suite sets it).
@end multitable

To see the resolved value of every variable, run:

@example
codebud config
@end example

To make a variable permanent, add it to your shell profile:

@example
echo 'export OLLAMA_MODEL=qwen2.5-coder:3b-instruct-q4_K_M' >> ~/.zshrc
@end example


@node Configuration File
@section Configuration File
@cindex configuration file
@cindex config.toml

Every setting in the table above can also be written in a TOML file, so
that it does not need exporting in each shell:

@example
# ~/.config/codebud/config.toml
model = "qwen2.5-coder:7b-instruct-q4_K_M"
num_ctx = 16384
rag_hints = false
@end example

The file lives at @file{$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/codebud/config.toml} (by default
@file{~/.config/codebud/config.toml}).  A project can have its own
@file{.codebud/config.toml}, found like its policy files
(@pxref{Project Policy}).  Where a value comes
from, highest first:

@enumerate
@item the environment variable;
@item the project's @file{.codebud/config.toml};
@item your @file{~/.config/codebud/config.toml};
@item the built-in default.
@end enumerate

@multitable @columnfractions .30 .32 .38
@headitem Key @tab Variable @tab Type
@item @code{backend} @tab @env{CODEBUD_BACKEND} @tab @code{"ollama"} / @code{"openai"} (user file only)
@item @code{model} @tab @env{OLLAMA_MODEL} @tab string
@item @code{ollama_base_url} @tab @env{OLLAMA_BASE_URL} @tab string (user file only)
@item @code{allow_remote_http} @tab @env{CODEBUD_ALLOW_REMOTE_HTTP} @tab @code{true} / @code{false} (user file only)
@item @code{embed_model} @tab @env{EMBED_MODEL} @tab string
@item @code{rag} @tab @env{CODEBUD_RAG} @tab @code{true} / @code{false}
@item @code{rag_hints} @tab @env{CODEBUD_RAG_HINTS} @tab @code{true} / @code{false}
@item @code{num_ctx} @tab @env{CODEBUD_NUM_CTX} @tab integer (user file only)
@item @code{openai_api_key} @tab @env{OPENAI_API_KEY} @tab string (user file only)
@item @code{openai_model} @tab @env{OPENAI_MODEL} @tab string (user file only)
@item @code{web_search} @tab @env{WEB_SEARCH_ENABLED} @tab @code{true} / @code{false} (user file only)
@item @code{mask_secrets} @tab @env{CODEBUD_MASK_SECRETS} @tab @code{true} / @code{false} (user file only)
@item @code{encrypt} @tab @env{CODEBUD_ENCRYPT} @tab @code{true} / @code{false} (user file only)
@item @code{step_env} @tab @env{CODEBUD_STEP_ENV} @tab names / @code{PREFIX_*}, space or comma separated (user file only)
@item @code{session_retention_days} @tab @env{CODEBUD_SESSION_RETENTION_DAYS} @tab integer (user file only)
@item @code{privacy} @tab @env{CODEBUD_PRIVACY} @tab @code{"locked"} / @code{"private"} / @code{"trusted"} (user file only)
@item @code{profile} @tab @env{CODEBUD_PROFILE} @tab a profile name (user file only)
@end multitable

@cindex egress settings
@strong{Settings that make data leave the machine} --- the backend, the
Ollama URL, plain-http opt-in, the OpenAI key and web search --- are
accepted only from your own file or the environment, and so are
@code{mask_secrets}, @code{encrypt}, @code{step_env},
@code{session_retention_days} and @code{privacy}, which protect your
data, and @code{num_ctx} (it sizes the model's memory use) and
@code{openai_model} (it decides what is billed to your OpenAI account).  In a project's file
they are ignored with a warning: cloning a repository must never be able
to send your prompts somewhere else or weaken your privacy.  The
same goes for unknown keys, values of the wrong type and a file that is not
valid TOML: Codebud warns once and carries on without them.

Keep secrets such as @code{openai_api_key} out of shared files, and make
your own file private (@command{chmod 600 ~/.config/codebud/config.toml}).

@command{codebud config} shows each value and where it came from
(@pxref{Checking Your Configuration}).  Python 3.10 needs the
@code{tomli} package to read the file (@pxref{Installation}); 3.11 and
later have it built in.


@node LLM Backend Variables
@section LLM Backend Variables
@cindex LLM backend
@cindex Ollama backend
@cindex OpenAI backend

@cindex CODEBUD_BACKEND
Codebud supports two LLM backends.  You choose one with
@env{CODEBUD_BACKEND} (or @code{backend} in @file{config.toml}); there is
no fallback from one to the other:

@table @code
@item ollama
The default.  Uses the Ollama server at @env{OLLAMA_BASE_URL} with
@env{OLLAMA_MODEL}.  Codebud streams tokens using @code{stream=True} so
that the HTTP connection never times out during the long prefill phase on
CPU-only hardware.

@item openai
Sends prompts to OpenAI with @env{OPENAI_API_KEY} and @env{OPENAI_MODEL}.
Needs the optional client: @code{pip install "codebud[openai]"}.  The call
is non-streaming and uses @code{temperature=0} for deterministic output.
Your prompts --- including project context and conversation history ---
leave this machine, so @command{run}, @command{plan} and @command{chat}
say so when they start, the chat toolbar shows @samp{☁ OpenAI}, and
@command{codebud doctor} names the backend.
@end table

Setting @env{OPENAI_API_KEY} alone does nothing: earlier versions sent
prompts to OpenAI by themselves whenever Ollama was unset or unreachable,
and that fallback is gone.  When the chosen backend cannot be used,
Codebud stops with a message saying why, for example:

@example
Plan error: Ollama is not reachable at http://localhost:11434. Prompts are
never sent anywhere else unless you choose CODEBUD_BACKEND=openai.
@end example

@noindent
A project's @file{.codebud/config.toml} cannot choose the backend
(@pxref{Configuration File}).

@subsection Pointing at a remote Ollama instance

@cindex remote Ollama
@cindex CODEBUD_ALLOW_REMOTE_HTTP
If you run Ollama on a separate machine (for example, a desktop with more
RAM), change @env{OLLAMA_BASE_URL}.  Your prompts then leave this machine,
so Codebud says so when @command{run}, @command{plan} or @command{chat}
starts, the chat toolbar shows the host next to the model, and
@command{codebud doctor} reports the backend as remote.

The connection should be encrypted.  Either serve Ollama over https
(behind a reverse proxy), or tunnel it over SSH and keep the URL local:

@example
ssh -N -L 11434:localhost:11434 desktop &      @r{# Ollama on "desktop"}
export OLLAMA_BASE_URL=http://localhost:11434  @r{# local end of the tunnel}
export OLLAMA_MODEL=qwen2.5-coder:7b-instruct-q4_K_M
codebud run "your task"
@end example

Codebud refuses a remote server over plain @code{http} --- it would carry
your code and prompts across the network unencrypted --- for planning,
chat replies and project search alike:

@example
Plan error: OLLAMA_BASE_URL http://192.168.1.50:11434 is another machine
over plain http: prompts would cross the network unencrypted. Use https,
or allow it with CODEBUD_ALLOW_REMOTE_HTTP=1 (...).
@end example

@noindent
On a network you trust, allow it explicitly with
@env{CODEBUD_ALLOW_REMOTE_HTTP}@code{=1}, or @code{allow_remote_http = true}
in your own @file{config.toml} (a project's file cannot set it, nor the
URL; @pxref{Configuration File}).  Codebud does not require the model to
be on the same machine as the CLI.

@cindex proxy
@cindex HTTP_PROXY
@subsection HTTP proxies

When @env{OLLAMA_BASE_URL} points at the local machine (@code{localhost},
@code{127.0.0.1} or @code{::1}), Codebud always connects directly and
ignores @env{HTTP_PROXY}, @env{HTTPS_PROXY} and @env{ALL_PROXY}.  A
system-wide proxy therefore never gets in the way of a local Ollama, even
without a @env{NO_PROXY} setting.

For a remote Ollama the usual proxy variables apply.  If the remote host
must be reached directly, list it in @env{NO_PROXY}:

@example
export NO_PROXY=gpu-box.example
@end example


@node Privacy Levels
@section Privacy Levels
@cindex privacy levels
@cindex CODEBUD_PRIVACY
@cindex locked
@cindex trusted

Two independent dials decide how Codebud behaves.  The @dfn{mode}
(@pxref{Agent Modes}) says how much it asks; the @dfn{privacy level}
says what it may reach:

@table @code
@item locked
Nothing leaves this machine.  Prompts and embeddings may only go to an
Ollama on this machine: the @code{openai} backend and a remote
@env{OLLAMA_BASE_URL} are refused with a message saying why.  Web search
is off, even with @env{WEB_SEARCH_ENABLED}.  Command steps run isolated
and may not use the network; where isolation is unavailable they are
refused.  For sensitive code and hostile networks.

@item private
The default.  Data leaves only where you chose --- the backend, a remote
Ollama, web search --- and each is announced, shown in
@command{codebud doctor}, and (for web search) confirmed in @code{ask}
and @code{auto} mode.  Command steps run isolated
(@pxref{Isolated Steps}).

@item trusted
For trusted environments and unattended work: command steps run
unisolated, and web searches and network steps without confirmation.
Announced when a command starts, shown in the chat toolbar and recorded
in the session file.
@end table

@noindent
@strong{Trust never lifts safety.}  The allow-list and the refused
arguments (@pxref{The Safety Engine}) apply at every level, and
powerful commands still ask in @code{auto} mode.

Fully unattended work is always possible: in @code{full-auto} mode
nothing asks, at the default @code{private} level too; @code{trusted}
additionally removes the remaining privacy prompts.

Choose the level with the global option @option{--privacy}, with
@code{privacy = "locked"} in your own @file{config.toml}, with
@env{CODEBUD_PRIVACY}, or in @command{chat} with
@code{/privacy @var{level}}.  A project's @file{config.toml} cannot set it.
A resumed session starts at the level you choose now, not the one it was
saved with.

@example
codebud --privacy locked chat                 @r{# for this session}
codebud --privacy trusted run --yes "..."     @r{# a trusted script}
@end example

@noindent
A profile sets the mode and the level together (@pxref{Profiles}).


@node Profiles
@section Profiles
@cindex profiles
@cindex CODEBUD_PROFILE
@cindex unattended profile

A @dfn{profile} names a combination of the two dials and what goes with
them: the mode (how much Codebud asks), the privacy level (what it may
reach), optionally a directory of policy files and extra instructions for
the planner.  Two are built in:

@table @code
@item default
@code{ask} mode, privacy @code{private}: Codebud asks before each step in
@command{chat}, and command steps run isolated.

@item unattended
@code{full-auto} mode, privacy @code{trusted}: nothing is asked ---
@command{run} and @command{plan --execute} behave as with @option{--yes}
--- and command steps are not isolated.  For CI jobs and machines you
trust.  The allow-list and the refused arguments still apply.
@end table

@noindent
Choose one with the global option @option{--profile}, with
@code{/profile @var{name}} in @command{chat}, with @env{CODEBUD_PROFILE},
or with @code{profile = "@var{name}"} in your own @file{config.toml}:

@example
codebud --profile unattended run "fix the failing test"
codebud profiles                    @r{# list them; * marks the active one}
@end example

@noindent
What you say explicitly still wins over the profile: @option{--privacy}
and @env{CODEBUD_PRIVACY} over its level, @option{--mode} over its mode,
@option{-i} over a @code{full-auto} profile.  The profile wins over
@code{privacy} in @file{config.toml}.  It is announced when a command
starts (unless it is @code{default}), shown in the chat toolbar and
recorded in the session file; like the privacy level, a resumed session
does not bring its profile back.

@subheading Your own profiles

A profile is a TOML file, @file{~/.config/codebud/profiles/@var{name}.toml}
(@env{XDG_CONFIG_HOME}); one named like a built-in replaces it.  Every
key is optional:

@example
description = "Review only: edit files, no commands, nothing leaves"
mode = "edit"                 # ask, auto, full-auto, plan, edit
privacy = "locked"            # locked, private, trusted
policy = "review-policy"      # directory, relative to this file
prompt = "Never modify files under tests/."
encrypt = true                # turn encryption at rest on
@end example

@noindent
@code{policy} names a directory holding any of the policy files
(@pxref{Policy JSON Files}); those it has are used first, before the
project's, yours and the shipped ones.  @code{prompt} is added to every
planner prompt.  @code{encrypt = true} turns encryption at rest on while
the profile is active, whatever the configuration says; a profile cannot
turn it off (@pxref{Encryption at Rest}).  A profile file with a mistake --- an unknown key, a mode
or level that does not exist, a missing policy directory --- is refused
with a message rather than half applied.  A project cannot define or
choose a profile, since a profile can lower the privacy level.


@node Isolated Steps
@section Isolated Steps
@cindex isolation
@cindex bubblewrap
@cindex network steps

At privacy level @code{private} (the default) and @code{locked}, every
@code{command} step runs inside a bubblewrap sandbox (@command{bwrap}, the
tool Flatpak uses), without root:

@itemize
@item no network --- unless the step declares @code{"network": true};
@item the whole file system read-only, except the project directory and a
fresh, private @file{/tmp};
@item your secrets hidden: @file{~/.ssh}, @file{~/.gnupg}, @file{~/.aws},
@file{~/.kube}, @file{~/.docker}, keyrings, @file{~/.password-store},
@file{.netrc}, @file{.pgpass}, @file{.pypirc}, @file{.npmrc},
@file{.git-credentials}, and Codebud's own data, configuration (with the
encryption key) and runtime directory (where a decrypted index copy
lives), wherever @env{XDG_DATA_HOME}, @env{XDG_CONFIG_HOME} and
@env{XDG_RUNTIME_DIR} put them;
@item your whole runtime directory (@file{/run/user/@var{uid}}) and the
system bus (@file{/run/dbus}): the sockets there --- the session D-Bus,
and through it systemd, which can start programs outside the sandbox; the
keyring; the gpg and SSH agents --- would let a step act outside it, and
connecting to a socket is not a write a read-only file system stops;
@item stopped together with Codebud.
@end itemize

@cindex environment of command steps
@cindex step_env
Hiding secret files is not enough if @command{printenv} can print a token
from the environment, so at every privacy level, @code{trusted} included,
a step does not inherit Codebud's environment.  It gets an allow-list:

@itemize
@item what ordinary tools need: @env{PATH}, @env{HOME}, @env{USER},
@env{LOGNAME}, @env{SHELL}, @env{LANG}, @env{LANGUAGE}, every
@env{LC_*}, @env{TZ}, @env{TERM}, @env{COLORTERM}, @env{NO_COLOR},
@env{FORCE_COLOR}, @env{EDITOR}, @env{VISUAL}, @env{PAGER},
@env{VIRTUAL_ENV}, @env{CONDA_PREFIX}, @env{CONDA_DEFAULT_ENV},
@env{PYTHONPATH}, @env{GOPATH}, @env{GOROOT}, @env{CARGO_HOME},
@env{RUSTUP_HOME}, @env{JAVA_HOME};
@item for a step that declared it needs the network, also the proxy
settings (@env{HTTP_PROXY}, @env{HTTPS_PROXY}, @env{ALL_PROXY},
@env{NO_PROXY}, in either case) and CA bundles (@env{SSL_CERT_FILE},
@env{SSL_CERT_DIR}, @env{REQUESTS_CA_BUNDLE}, @env{CURL_CA_BUNDLE});
@item what you add with @code{step_env}: names or @code{PREFIX_*}
patterns, separated by spaces or commas.
@end itemize

@noindent
Everything else is left out: @env{GITHUB_TOKEN}, @env{AWS_*}, the SSH
agent (@env{SSH_AUTH_SOCK}), @env{TMPDIR} (a step has its own
@file{/tmp}).  The encryption passphrase and Codebud's secret settings
(@env{OPENAI_API_KEY}) are never passed, even if listed.  If a tool a
step runs needs a variable, add it in your own @file{config.toml}:

@example
step_env = "MY_TOOL_HOME, JFROG_*"
@end example

@noindent
A project's @file{.codebud/config.toml} cannot set @code{step_env}: a
cloned repository must not be able to hand your tokens to its commands.

A step that needs the internet --- @command{pip install},
@command{git fetch} --- declares it (the planner is told how), and is then
shown and confirmed like a web search: in @code{ask} and @code{auto} mode
and in @command{codebud run} (unless @option{--yes}); in
@code{full-auto} it runs unattended.  At @code{locked} such steps are
refused.  At @code{trusted} steps are not isolated at all.

Tools that write outside the project, such as a package manager's cache
in @file{~/.cache}, fail inside the sandbox; run them at @code{trusted}
or outside Codebud.

@subheading Where isolation is not available

Without bubblewrap, @command{unshare} (util-linux) still cuts the network,
but leaves the file system unprotected --- and a step that declares it
needs the network then has no isolation at all, which Codebud warns about
once per run (at @code{locked} such steps are refused anyway).  Which
isolation works is detected once, when the first step runs; restart
Codebud after installing bubblewrap.  Where neither works --- macOS,
the BSDs, containers, systems that block unprivileged user namespaces
(older Debian, Ubuntu 24.04 and later without an AppArmor profile for
@command{bwrap}) --- steps are not isolated: at @code{private} Codebud
warns once and runs them; at @code{locked} it refuses command steps.
@command{codebud doctor} shows which applies:

@example
  [PASS] Isolation: bubblewrap: no network, project-only writes, secrets hidden
@end example

Install bubblewrap with your package manager (@command{pacman -S
bubblewrap}, @command{apt install bubblewrap}, @command{dnf install
bubblewrap}).


@node Feature Flags
@section Feature Flags
@cindex feature flags
@cindex web search, enabling

@table @env
@item WEB_SEARCH_ENABLED
Default: @code{0} (disabled).  Set to @code{1} (or @code{true} or
@code{yes}) to enable the @code{web_search} tool.  When enabled, the tool
sends a POST request to @code{https://lite.duckduckgo.com/lite/} and
parses the HTML response.  No API key is required.

@example
WEB_SEARCH_ENABLED=1 codebud run "find the latest Python release notes"
@end example

When disabled, the @code{web_search} tool's description in the planner
prompt is replaced with a notice so the planner knows not to use it.
When enabled, every query is shown and, except in @code{full-auto} mode,
confirmed before it is sent (@pxref{web_search}).

@item CODEBUD_RAG
Default: @code{1}.  When the project has an index (@pxref{The index
Subcommand}), each request's most relevant chunks are added to the
planning prompt.  Set to @code{0} to plan without them (or pass
@option{--no-rag}, or type @code{/rag off} in @command{chat}), e.g.@: to
compare results:

@example
CODEBUD_RAG=0 codebud plan "add a docstring to compact_history"
@end example
@end table


@node Policy JSON Files
@section Policy JSON Files
@cindex policy files

Three JSON files make up the safety policy that the @code{SafetyEngine}
loads each time @code{AgentCore} starts:

@table @file
@item harmless_commands.json
Commands that only read or report.  @xref{Harmless Commands}.

@item powerful_commands.json
Commands that run code or change files.  They are permitted, but in
@code{auto} mode and in @command{codebud run} (unless @option{--yes}) each
one needs an explicit confirmation.  @xref{Powerful Commands}.

@item trusted_sequences.json
Named multi-step sequences that the planner may propose as a unit.
@xref{Trusted Sequences}.
@end table

Your policy is, for each file, the first one found of:

@enumerate
@item
the active profile's policy directory, if it has one (@pxref{Profiles});
@item
@file{$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/codebud/@var{name}} (by default
@file{~/.config/codebud/@var{name}}): your own policy for all projects;
@item
the defaults shipped with Codebud.
@end enumerate

@noindent
A project's @file{.codebud/} can then adjust it (@pxref{Project Policy}).
To change your policy, copy a default file to @file{~/.config/codebud/}
and edit the copy; do not edit the shipped defaults.
@command{codebud config} shows which files are in effect, and whether a
project policy is trusted (@pxref{Checking Your Configuration}).

Entries must be plain command words (@samp{git status}, @samp{python3}):
anything else is dropped with a warning, from every file, since the lists
are also shown to the model.

All three files must be valid JSON arrays.  If a file is malformed, the
@code{SafetyEngine} treats that list as empty and logs a warning;
@command{codebud doctor} validates the files in effect.

@menu
* Project Policy::         What a project's .codebud/ may change.
@end menu

@node Project Policy
@subsection Project Policy
@cindex project policy
@cindex .codebud directory
@cindex trust, project
@cindex trust subcommand

A project can carry its own @file{.codebud/harmless_commands.json},
@file{powerful_commands.json} and @file{trusted_sequences.json}, for its
own tools (@command{make test}, @command{cargo build}, @dots{}).  Since a
repository you clone may come from anyone, a project policy can always
@emph{narrow} yours but only @emph{widen} it once you trust it:

@itemize
@item
@strong{Narrowing applies at once.}  A command the project leaves out of
both its lists is not allowed in it; one it lists as powerful is asked
about there even if you call it harmless.
@item
@strong{Widening waits for your trust.}  A new command, a powerful
command made harmless or a new trusted sequence is ignored until you
trust the project.  @command{run}, @command{plan} and @command{chat}
show what it would add --- flagging shells, interpreters, network tools
and the like --- and ask once; without a terminal they ignore it with a
notice.  @command{codebud trust} does the same on demand,
@option{--yes} without asking, @option{--revoke} withdraws it.
@item
@strong{Trust is tied to the files.}  It is recorded in
@file{~/.config/codebud/trusted-projects.json} with the SHA-256 of the
project's policy files, so any change to them --- by a @command{git pull},
a teammate, or one of Codebud's own steps --- needs trusting again.
@end itemize

@noindent
The @file{.codebud/} directory is looked for from where Codebud starts up
to the top of its git work tree (outside git, in that directory only),
never in your home directory or @file{/}; one owned by another user is
ignored with a warning, so a @file{/tmp/.codebud/} someone else made does
not apply to your projects.  File steps can never write into
@file{.codebud/} (@pxref{What Is Not Protected}).

@node Harmless Commands
@section Harmless Commands
@cindex harmless_commands.json

@file{harmless_commands.json} lists commands that only read or
report.  A @code{command} step is accepted when its leading words match an
entry exactly (@pxref{The Safety Engine}): @code{"ls"} allows
@code{ls -la} but not @code{lsblk}, and @code{"git log"} allows
@code{git log -n 3} but not @code{git push}.

Default content:

@example
[
  "echo", "cat", "head", "tail", "ls", "pwd", "whoami", "wc",
  "find", "diff", "git log", "git status", "git diff", "git show"
]
@end example

Some arguments are refused even for listed programs, for example
@code{find -exec} and @code{find -delete} (@pxref{The Safety Engine}).

@strong{Note:} be specific with multi-word tools.  Adding @code{"git"}
would permit @emph{all} git subcommands, including @command{git push} and
@command{git reset --hard}; list @code{"git log"}, @code{"git status"} and
so on separately instead.  Do not put programs that run code
(interpreters, test runners, build tools) here; they belong in the
powerful list.


@node Powerful Commands
@section Powerful Commands
@cindex powerful_commands.json

@file{powerful_commands.json} lists commands that run code or
change files.  They are permitted, but they are not trusted blindly: in
@code{auto} mode, where a plan otherwise runs after one approval, Codebud
stops before each powerful step and asks @samp{Run it? [y/N]}
(@pxref{Agent Modes}).  @command{codebud run} asks the same unless it is
given @option{--yes}.  In @code{ask} mode every step is confirmed anyway;
@code{full-auto} runs them without asking.

Default content:

@example
[
  "grep", "sed", "awk", "cp", "mv",
  "python", "python3", "pytest", "ruff"
]
@end example

@code{python}, @code{python3} and @code{pytest} execute arbitrary code (a
script, a test suite's @file{conftest.py}); @code{ruff} rewrites files with
@option{--fix} or @command{ruff format}.  Inline code (@code{python -c}) is
refused outright.

Common additions for a developer workflow:

@example
[
  "grep", "sed", "awk", "cp", "mv",
  "python", "python3", "pytest", "ruff",
  "mkdir", "touch", "chmod",
  "pip install", "npm install", "git add", "git commit"
]
@end example

@strong{Warning:} do not add @code{"rm"}, @code{"sudo"}, or
@code{"shutdown"} to either list.  The most destructive forms
(@code{rm -rf /}, powering off, erasing a disk) are refused even if you
do (@pxref{The Sandbox}), but everything short of them would run.


@node Trusted Sequences
@section Trusted Sequences
@cindex trusted_sequences.json

@file{trusted_sequences.json} is a JSON array of named
multi-step sequences.  Each entry has a @code{name} and a @code{sequence}
of command prefixes:

@example
[
  @{
    "name": "install_python_package",
    "sequence": ["python3 -m pip install"]
  @}
]
@end example

The @code{SafetyEngine} loads this file but the current implementation does
not apply sequence-level validation beyond what the step-by-step check
already covers.  The file is reserved for future expansion --- for example,
requiring that @command{pip install} is always followed by a test run
before any commit.


@node Checking Your Configuration
@section Checking Your Configuration
@cindex codebud config
@cindex codebud doctor

Two subcommands help verify your configuration:

@table @command
@item codebud config
Prints the value of every setting and where it came from
(@samp{environment}, @samp{project config}, @samp{user config} or
@samp{default}; @pxref{Configuration File}), followed by the configuration
files, the data directory and the policy file in effect for each name.
Use this to confirm that your settings are being read correctly.

@example
codebud config
@end example

Example output:

@example
Codebud resolved configuration:
  OLLAMA_MODEL              = qwen2.5-coder:3b-instruct-q4_K_M   (environment)
  OLLAMA_BASE_URL           = http://localhost:11434             (default)
  EMBED_MODEL               = all-minilm                         (default)
  CODEBUD_RAG               = 1                                  (default)
  CODEBUD_RAG_HINTS         = 1                                  (default)
  CODEBUD_NUM_CTX           = 16384                              (user config)
  OPENAI_API_KEY            = (not set)                          (default)
  OPENAI_MODEL              = gpt-3.5-turbo                      (default)
  WEB_SEARCH_ENABLED        = 0                                  (default)

Files:
  config file              = /home/you/.config/codebud/config.toml
  data directory           = /home/you/.local/share/codebud
  harmless_commands.json   = /home/you/.config/codebud/harmless_commands.json
  powerful_commands.json   = /usr/local/lib/python3.14/site-packages/codebud/data/policy/powerful_commands.json
  trusted_sequences.json   = /usr/local/lib/python3.14/site-packages/codebud/data/policy/trusted_sequences.json
@end example

@item codebud doctor
Checks each configuration value for correctness: whether the Ollama server
is reachable, whether the selected model has been pulled, and whether the
three policy JSON files parse without errors.  Each check prints
@samp{[PASS]} or @samp{[FAIL]} with a one-line fix command; optional
items, such as a missing embedding model, print @samp{[WARN]} and do not
count as failures.

@example
codebud doctor
@end example
@end table


@node Files and Directories
@section Files and Directories
@cindex XDG directories
@cindex data directory
@cindex .codebud directory

Codebud's locations do not depend on the directory it is started from.
A relative @env{XDG_DATA_HOME}, @env{XDG_CONFIG_HOME} or
@env{XDG_RUNTIME_DIR} is ignored, with a warning, as the XDG Base
Directory specification requires.

@multitable @columnfractions .38 .62
@headitem Location @tab Contents
@item @file{$XDG_DATA_HOME/codebud/} (default @file{~/.local/share/codebud/})
@tab Runtime data: @file{memory/entries.jsonl} (execution history),
@file{sessions/} (chat sessions), @file{index/} (project indexes,
@pxref{The index Subcommand}).
@item @file{$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/codebud/} (default @file{~/.config/codebud/})
@tab Your @file{config.toml}, policy files (overriding the shipped
defaults), @file{profiles/}, @file{trusted-projects.json} (projects whose
policy you trust), and @file{keys/}: the encryption key, sealed
with your passphrase (@pxref{Encryption at Rest}).
@item @file{.codebud/} in a project
@tab That project's @file{config.toml} and policy files, which narrow
yours and widen it only once you trust the project (@pxref{Project
Policy}).
@end multitable

@cindex permissions, data files
The runtime data holds your execution history, your conversations and
copies of your project's text, so Codebud keeps it private: every
directory it creates is mode 0700 and every file 0600 (owner only),
whatever your @code{umask}.  @command{codebud doctor} warns when anything
in the data or configuration directory is readable by other users ---
for example data written by an older version --- and gives the command
that fixes it:

@example
chmod -R go-rwx ~/.local/share/codebud
@end example

@cindex secret masking
@cindex [redacted]
Secrets are masked before anything is stored: in saved chat sessions and
in the step history, these become @samp{[redacted]}:

@itemize
@item API keys and tokens in well-known formats: OpenAI and Anthropic
@samp{sk-@dots{}}, Stripe @samp{sk_live_@dots{}}, GitHub, GitLab
@samp{glpat-@dots{}}, npm, Hugging Face @samp{hf_@dots{}}, AWS, Slack
(tokens and webhook URLs), Google (API keys and OAuth @samp{ya29.@dots{}}),
SendGrid, JWTs, and Codebud's own age keys
(@samp{AGE-SECRET-KEY-@dots{}});
@item private-key blocks (PEM, OpenSSH, PGP), whole --- to the end of the
output when the @samp{END} line is missing, as with @command{head};
@item credentials in context: @samp{Authorization:} headers and
@samp{Bearer} tokens, passwords in URLs (even ones containing @samp{@@}),
@option{--password=@dots{}} style options, @code{mysql -p@var{secret}},
@code{curl -u @var{user}:@var{password}}, Azure @samp{AccountKey=},
Docker @samp{"auth"} values and @file{.pgpass} lines;
@item values given to secret-looking names: quoted literals
(@code{api_key = "@dots{}"}, @code{password: '@dots{}'}) and unquoted
values in INI, YAML and @file{.env} lines (@code{aws_secret_access_key =
@dots{}}, @code{password: @dots{}}, @code{DB_PASSWORD=@dots{}}).
@end itemize

@noindent
The names stay, so the history still reads.  Ordinary code is left alone:
a value that is a call (@code{token = get_token()}), an attribute
(@code{password = self.password}), a number, a boolean, a
@code{$@{@dots{}@}} or @code{@{@{@dots{}@}@}} reference or a path is not
a secret.  Values shorter than 8 characters are not masked, since too
many ordinary words would be.  Prompts sent to the model are not changed --- only what is
written to disk --- so a resumed session shows @samp{[redacted]} where
the original had a secret.  The project index is masked too: secret
files are never indexed, and secrets inside ordinary files are masked
before they are stored (@pxref{The index Subcommand}).  Set @env{CODEBUD_MASK_SECRETS} to
@samp{0} (or @code{mask_secrets = false} in your own
@file{config.toml}) to store everything verbatim.

Versions before October 2026 kept runtime data in a @file{data/} directory
inside the source checkout.  On first start, Codebud copies anything found
there into the data directory and reports how many files it copied; the
originals are left in place, and you can delete @file{data/} once you have
checked the new location.  Symbolic links there are never followed.  With
encryption at rest on, the copies are encrypted as Codebud would write
them (an old @file{entries.json} becomes sealed @file{entries.jsonl}
lines); if encryption is on but has no key yet, nothing is copied until
it has (@pxref{Encryption at Rest}).

